<h2>Why the shared household is exposed</h2>
<p>Section 17 declares that every woman in a domestic relationship has the right to reside in the shared household, whether or not she has any right, title or beneficial interest in it, and that she shall not be evicted or excluded except in accordance with the procedure established by law. The strength of the provision — that it does not depend on ownership — is also its exposure: because the woman typically holds no title, the title-holder retains the formal power to deal with the property. The Act&rsquo;s answer is not to freeze ownership generally but to give the Magistrate targeted restraining powers, exercisable case by case.</p>

<h2>The Section 19 menu of restraints</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>What the Magistrate may order</th><th>What it guards against</th></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(a)</td><td>Restrain dispossession or disturbance of the aggrieved person&rsquo;s possession</td><td>Lock-outs and forced removal</td></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(b)</td><td>Direct the respondent to remove himself from the shared household</td><td>Continued abuse within the home</td></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(c)</td><td>Restrain the respondent or his relatives from entering the portion where the aggrieved person resides</td><td>Harassment within a divided household</td></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(d)</td><td>Restrain alienation, disposal or encumbrance of the shared household</td><td>Sale, gift or mortgage during the case</td></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(e)</td><td>Restrain renunciation of rights in the shared household except with the court&rsquo;s leave</td><td>Surrender of tenancy, relinquishment deeds</td></tr>
  <tr><td>S.19(1)(f)</td><td>Direct alternate accommodation of the same level, or payment of its rent</td><td>Situations where residence in the household is no longer practicable</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two features deserve emphasis. First, clauses (d) and (e) are aimed squarely at transactions, not conduct — Parliament foresaw the disappearing-house problem. Second, by Section 23 the Magistrate may grant any Section 19 relief on an interim basis, and ex parte where the affidavit discloses that domestic violence has occurred or is likely; a restraint on alienation is most valuable precisely when it arrives before the transfer, which is usually the first hearing, not the last.</p>

<h2>The race against the registry</h2>
<p>For the aggrieved person, the practical question is speed and specificity. An application that seeks only &ldquo;protection and residence orders&rdquo; in general terms invites a general order; an application that identifies the property, pleads the apprehension of transfer with its basis — a broker&rsquo;s visit, a listing, loose talk of sale, a demand that she vacate — and asks in terms for restraints under Sections 19(1)(d) and (e) invites an order that can be acted upon. Where an interim restraint is granted, counsel should consider seeking a direction that the order be communicated to the jurisdictional Sub-Registrar, so that the restraint is visible at the point where a transfer would be registered. The order should also be served on any housing society or landlord whose cooperation a transfer or surrender would need.</p>

<h2>When the transfer has already happened</h2>
<p>A transfer executed before any restraint existed presents the hard case. The proceedings continue against the respondent, and the woman&rsquo;s remedies adjust rather than evaporate: the alternate accommodation clause, Section 19(1)(f), lets the court shift the obligation from the specific house to an equivalent roof or its rent; the monetary relief provisions capture the loss; and a transfer made during proceedings, to a close relative, for inadequate consideration, wears its purpose on its face and invites close judicial scrutiny. Whether a particular transferee is bound is a fact-dependent contest — turning on notice, consideration and bona fides — which is exactly why prevention under clause (d) is worth more than cure.</p>

<h2>The respondent&rsquo;s side: legitimate dealings under a cloud</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>A restraint order is not an expropriation.</strong> Title remains with the owner; what is suspended is the power to deal with the property so as to defeat the residence claim.</li>
  <li><strong>Genuine necessity has a route:</strong> Section 19(1)(e) contemplates leave of the Magistrate. A respondent who truly must sell — to meet medical costs, discharge debt, or fund a settlement — should apply for leave with full disclosure, often offering to secure alternate accommodation or deposit part of the proceeds.</li>
  <li><strong>Transparency protects the transaction.</strong> A sale negotiated openly, at market value, with provision made for the aggrieved person, stands on an entirely different footing from a midnight gift deed to a brother.</li>
  <li><strong>Family property is not automatically immune.</strong> Whether a particular house owned by relatives is a shared household is often the real contest; that question should be fought on pleadings and evidence, not by transferring the property while it is pending.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In Delhi practice, the alienation restraint is among the most under-sought reliefs in DV applications — and among the most regretted omissions. Aggrieved persons&rsquo; counsel should treat Sections 19(1)(d) and (e) as standard prayers whenever a residence claim is made over property the respondent controls. Respondents&rsquo; counsel should remember the mirror rule: a dealing with the shared household during proceedings, without leave, converts a defensible property decision into evidence of intent.</p>
</div>
